00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:54.360
So what is a matrix?

00:10:54.360 --> 00:11:00.180
I defined a vector as being an
ordered list of numbers.

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:05.880
think of a matrix as a
rectangular array of

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:06.880
numbers.

00:11:11.740 --> 00:11:15.360
The letter I will use, or the
sort of notation I will use for

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:19.500
matrices is I will refer to them
by capital letters, no arrow hat

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:20.840
on top.

00:11:20.840 --> 00:11:24.760
And we will use square brackets
like we did for vectors.

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:27.120
So I'm just going to use the
same example I've used in the

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:29.400
notes here for our first vector.

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:32.320
3, 2, 0, 2 is its first column,

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:33.500
1, 1, negative 1,

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:35.440
negative 2 is its second column,

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:39.640
and 4, 9, 0, 0 is its third
column.

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:44.860
Now, this matrix has four rows
and three columns.

00:11:46.100 --> 00:11:48.880
And the default interpretation I
want you to have when you look

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:51.880
at a matrix immediately is that
you should just think of it as

00:11:51.880 --> 00:11:54.720
being a collection of vectors
written next to each other.

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:59.660
OK, so you can imagine this is
like the vector A1,

00:11:59.660 --> 00:12:02.660
this is the vector A2,

00:12:02.660 --> 00:12:05.720
maybe this is the vector A3,

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:06.640
OK?

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:09.020
But there are two
interpretations.

00:12:09.020 --> 00:12:14.270
I can think of this as three
vectors in R4,

00:12:20.080 --> 00:12:22.920
or what else could I think of
this as? And this relates to a

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:24.880
question that was in homework
four.

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:27.460
Four vectors in R3? Yeah, just
as well.

00:12:27.460 --> 00:12:32.710
I can think of this as four
vectors in R3.

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:40.680
OK, great.

00:12:41.500 --> 00:12:45.040
So this matrix is four rows and
three columns.

00:12:45.040 --> 00:12:48.260
So the way that I would describe
its shape

00:12:48.260 --> 00:12:50.540
is four by three.
And this is how I pronounce

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:52.820
that.
4 by 3.

00:12:53.800 --> 00:12:59.720
And I might say that this matrix
is in the set R 4

00:12:59.720 --> 00:13:01.160
by 3,

00:13:01.160 --> 00:13:05.280
which you can think of as the
set of all 4 by 3 matrices whose

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:08.280
components are real numbers,

00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:09.540
OK?

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:13.590
Rows by columns.

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:21.430
Another piece of notation here
just to be aware of,

00:13:21.960 --> 00:13:26.460
sometimes if we have a matrix,

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:30.880
next to it we may write the
subscripts i,

00:13:30.880 --> 00:13:31.340
j,

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:33.500
and sometimes we'll use
uppercase letters, sometimes

00:13:33.500 --> 00:13:35.440
we'll use lowercase letters.

00:13:35.440 --> 00:13:40.690
All this is referring to is the
element

00:13:41.080 --> 00:13:43.330
at row I,

00:13:44.020 --> 00:13:45.520
column J.

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:50.140
So whenever referring to
matrices,

00:13:50.140 --> 00:13:54.080
we always index through the rows
first and then the columns,

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:55.080
OK?

00:13:57.720 --> 00:13:59.970
So for example,

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:04.160
let's just save some space here.

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:04.880
Also,

