00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:14.200
We'll talk more about that in
just a bit So anyways here, this

00:16:14.200 --> 00:16:16.660
is a matrix.
That is four rows by three

00:16:16.660 --> 00:16:19.660
columns in general So,

00:16:21.580 --> 00:16:27.400
n has been

00:16:27.400 --> 00:16:30.220
the placeholder I've used for
the number of things in a

00:16:30.220 --> 00:16:31.220
vector,

00:16:31.440 --> 00:16:33.900
and so it'll be the placeholder
I use for the number of rows in

00:16:33.900 --> 00:16:34.940
a matrix.

00:16:34.940 --> 00:16:39.320
D will be the placeholder I use
for the number of columns in a

00:16:39.320 --> 00:16:40.320
matrix,

00:16:40.320 --> 00:16:41.820
okay? OK,

00:16:46.660 --> 00:16:52.580
so based on n and d, which are
the matrix's shape,

00:16:52.580 --> 00:16:55.420
I'm not going to call it the
matrix's dimensions because

00:16:55.420 --> 00:16:57.340
dimension has a very specific
meaning.

00:16:57.340 --> 00:17:00.640
It's the number of vectors in a
basis for a subspace.

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:04.500
So to avoid confusing it with
that word, I'm going to call it

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:06.000
the shape,

00:17:06.120 --> 00:17:07.120
OK?

00:17:07.520 --> 00:17:12.620
A matrix who has more rows than
columns,

00:17:12.620 --> 00:17:17.120
we often call a tall matrix.

00:17:19.030 --> 00:17:24.960
A matrix who has more columns
than rows, we call a

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:26.460
