00:17:27.420 --> 00:17:31.860
And a matrix who has an equal
number of rows and columns,

00:17:31.860 --> 00:17:36.340
what do you think we call it?

00:17:36.340 --> 00:17:40.090
A square matrix, okay good.

00:17:40.580 --> 00:17:45.320
Now as we will see throughout
the semester,

00:17:45.800 --> 00:17:48.680
square matrices are special and
there are a lot of things that

00:17:48.680 --> 00:17:51.940
really only apply to square
matrices.

00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:56.440
But everything we do today will
apply to matrices in general.

00:17:56.760 --> 00:18:00.260
And sometimes non-square
matrices are called rectangular.

00:18:00.900 --> 00:18:01.900
Okay,

00:18:02.040 --> 00:18:05.640
now if you want to think in the
context of collecting real data

00:18:05.640 --> 00:18:07.140
in which,

00:18:07.760 --> 00:18:11.940
suppose I ran a survey or in
general just collected some

00:18:11.940 --> 00:18:17.420
features about various
individuals and had one row per

00:18:17.420 --> 00:18:22.180
data point and one column per
feature, what type of matrix do

00:18:22.180 --> 00:18:25.380
you think that information would
be stored in? A tall matrix, a

00:18:25.380 --> 00:18:29.680
wide matrix, or a square matrix?
You've got to guess.

00:18:29.680 --> 00:18:32.320
Like real data that are
typically

00:18:32.320 --> 00:18:35.860
stored in a wide, tall, or
square matrix.

00:18:35.860 --> 00:18:38.860
What do you think?

00:18:40.400 --> 00:18:41.400
Yeah?

00:18:41.800 --> 00:18:44.400
I would say definitely tall.
You probably would have a lot of

00:18:44.400 --> 00:18:45.940
data points and not that many
columns.

00:18:45.940 --> 00:18:46.940
Exactly.

00:18:47.140 --> 00:18:50.340
Usually, data that comes from
measuring some real world thing

00:18:50.340 --> 00:18:53.980
is tall in that we have lots of
data points where n is the

00:18:53.980 --> 00:18:56.720
number of data points we have.
That is the same placeholder I

00:18:56.720 --> 00:18:59.020
use for the number of data
points in a data set when we

00:18:59.020 --> 00:19:01.020
were doing simple linear
regression and the constant

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:01.880
model.

00:19:01.880 --> 00:19:06.380
And maybe not as many different
measurements per data point.

00:19:06.380 --> 00:19:09.380
Okay, so that means real data
often is stored in a tall

00:19:09.380 --> 00:19:10.380
matrix.

00:19:10.820 --> 00:19:11.560
Okay,

00:19:11.560 --> 00:19:16.060
one little thing I want to
remark on rows versus columns.

00:19:17.160 --> 00:19:19.060
What I'm writing here,

00:19:19.060 --> 00:19:20.060
174.
Therefore,

00:19:20.500 --> 00:19:25.340
this is a vector in R3.

00:19:27.800 --> 00:19:32.600
It is also a matrix

00:19:32.600 --> 00:19:36.350
with shape 3 by 1.

00:19:38.680 --> 00:19:42.430
These are the same thing.

00:19:46.480 --> 00:19:50.230
Sound reasonable? A vector is
just a special case of a matrix.

00:19:50.860 --> 00:19:54.610
Now, what about this creature?

00:19:57.200 --> 00:19:59.600
It has the same three numbers,
1, 7, and 4,

00:19:59.600 --> 00:20:01.900
but they are written sideways
rather than on top of each

00:20:01.900 --> 00:20:02.900
