00:20:05.480 --> 00:20:09.960
This is a matrix with shape
what?

00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:11.580
It's number of rows by number of
columns.

00:20:11.580 --> 00:20:13.140
What would we say the shape of
this thing is?

00:20:13.140 --> 00:20:13.900
Yeah,

00:20:13.900 --> 00:20:15.580
1 by 3.

00:20:15.580 --> 00:20:19.220
I'm not going to say this is a
vector in R3.

00:20:19.220 --> 00:20:22.720
Okay, vectors in R3 and general
vectors in Rn by convention in

00:20:22.720 --> 00:20:26.080
our class will refer to column
vectors.

00:20:26.080 --> 00:20:26.460
Okay,

00:20:26.460 --> 00:20:30.140
so if I wanted to refer to this
I would just say it's a 1 by 3

00:20:30.140 --> 00:20:34.680
matrix or I might call it a row
vector

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:39.060
not

00:20:39.060 --> 00:20:43.500
a vector in R3.

00:20:45.440 --> 00:20:50.690
Okay, so 174 is a column vector
and 174 is a row vector.

00:20:50.700 --> 00:20:54.300
Those are distinct quantities.
I mean the are the same,

00:20:54.820 --> 00:20:58.570
but there are different objects
mathematically.

00:21:02.500 --> 00:21:04.000
Sound OK?

00:21:04.380 --> 00:21:07.520
And at the end of today, I'll
try and show you this operation

00:21:07.520 --> 00:21:11.020
that we can apply to a column
vector to turn it into a row

00:21:11.020 --> 00:21:12.220
vector and vice versa.

00:21:12.220 --> 00:21:16.760
But that'll also be defined more
generally in terms of matrices

00:21:16.760 --> 00:21:18.260
as well.
It's not just about vectors.

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:20.060
OK,

00:21:20.060 --> 00:21:22.920
so we know what a matrix is.
You can think of it as just like

00:21:22.920 --> 00:21:26.670
an extension of vectors, where
it's like a vector of vectors.

00:21:26.820 --> 00:21:28.860
It's a vector made up of column
vectors,

00:21:28.860 --> 00:21:29.860
OK?

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:34.240
What are the operations this new
object needs to support?

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:37.000
Well, it supports addition and
scalar multiplication,

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:41.500
which works exactly the same way
you would think it does.

00:21:43.260 --> 00:21:46.500
So let's suppose I have the same
example A from before.

00:21:46.500 --> 00:21:47.160
3,

00:21:47.160 --> 00:21:48.320
1, 4,

00:21:48.320 --> 00:21:50.240
2, 1, 9,

00:21:50.240 --> 00:21:53.240
0, negative 1, 0,

00:21:54.440 --> 00:21:57.040
2, negative 2, 0.

00:21:57.040 --> 00:21:59.840
By the way, and for what we're
about to do on the next page,

00:21:59.840 --> 00:22:01.660
this will become a little more
important.

00:22:01.660 --> 00:22:05.220
I like to sometimes write the
shape of a matrix in the bottom

00:22:05.220 --> 00:22:08.900
right corner just so I can keep
track of what operations are

00:22:08.900 --> 00:22:09.900
allowed.

00:22:09.980 --> 00:22:10.980
OK?

00:22:11.560 --> 00:22:13.880
For addition and scalar
multiplication, In addition,

00:22:13.880 --> 00:22:18.340
what do you think the shape of B
must be in order to add A and B,

00:22:18.340 --> 00:22:19.000
let's say?

00:22:19.000 --> 00:22:22.200
What do you think the shape of B
must be?

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:23.380
So what then?

00:22:23.380 --> 00:22:23.800
Yeah,

00:22:23.800 --> 00:22:27.680
the shape of B needs to be the
same as the shape of A,

00:22:27.680 --> 00:22:28.160
OK,

00:22:28.160 --> 00:22:30.220
in order for you to be able to
add matrices,

00:22:30.220 --> 00:22:30.940
OK?

00:22:30.940 --> 00:22:33.800
And that's because the way you
add is element-wise, the same

00:22:33.800 --> 00:22:35.300
way you added vectors,

00:22:35.300 --> 00:22:37.960
OK? So imagine, let's just keep
B simple,

00:22:37.960 --> 00:22:39.040
1, 2, 3,

00:22:39.040 --> 00:22:42.460
4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9,

00:22:42.460 --> 00:22:42.960
10,

00:22:42.960 --> 00:22:44.460
11, 12,

00:22:44.480 --> 00:22:48.340
a valid operation is something
like 3 times A minus B.

00:22:48.680 --> 00:22:53.660
That'll give me a new matrix
whose shape is also 4 by 3,

00:22:54.340 --> 00:22:59.400
whose elements will come from
taking 3 times an element in A

00:22:59.400 --> 00:23:02.220
minus the corresponding element
in B.

00:23:02.220 --> 00:23:04.980
Okay, we don't need to sit here
doing all of these, But,

00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:10.080
why don't you tell me the
element at position 1 comma 3,

00:23:10.680 --> 00:23:11.900
row 1 column 3.

00:23:11.900 --> 00:23:15.650
What's that going to be?

00:23:17.400 --> 00:23:18.840
Anyone know?

00:23:18.840 --> 00:23:19.620
Yeah, Chris.

00:23:19.620 --> 00:23:20.240
9.
Good.

00:23:20.240 --> 00:23:21.360
How'd you get that?

00:23:21.360 --> 00:23:23.600
Times 3 minus 3.

00:23:23.600 --> 00:23:26.220
Good.
4 times 3 minus 3 gives you 9.

00:23:27.740 --> 00:23:29.540
And let's just do one more.

00:23:29.540 --> 00:23:34.560
So what about the element at
position 3 comma 2?

00:23:35.260 --> 00:23:39.010
What do we get there?

00:23:39.200 --> 00:23:40.780
Negative 11.
Negative 11.

00:23:40.780 --> 00:23:44.100
And you got that by doing
negative 1 times 3, which is

00:23:44.100 --> 00:23:48.720
negative 3 minus 8, which gives
you negative 11.

00:23:49.920 --> 00:23:50.920
OK.

00:23:51.000 --> 00:23:53.040
That much is pretty
straightforward.

00:23:53.040 --> 00:23:56.860
What's about to happen for the
rest of today is not really as

00:23:56.860 --> 00:23:58.820
straightforward as this.
Okay, because matrix

00:23:58.820 --> 00:24:02.040
multiplication does not really
work the way that you would

00:24:02.040 --> 00:24:03.240
expect it to.

00:24:03.240 --> 00:24:03.700
Okay,

00:24:03.700 --> 00:24:06.480
that like if you had to guess
based on this slide, how do you

00:24:06.480 --> 00:24:08.840
multiply matrices A and B,

00:24:08.840 --> 00:24:10.020
one would probably think,

00:24:10.020 --> 00:24:12.920
oh, you just do 3 times 1, that
gives you 3, and 1 times 2, that

00:24:12.920 --> 00:24:15.740
gives you 2, and 4 times 3 gives
you 12, and 2 times 4 gives you

00:24:15.740 --> 00:24:16.200
8.

00:24:16.200 --> 00:24:18.260
Okay, you multiply the
corresponding element.

00:24:18.260 --> 00:24:20.510
That's not matrix
multiplication.

00:24:20.820 --> 00:24:23.780
Okay, that is not matrix
multiplication.

00:24:23.780 --> 00:24:26.030
Unfortunately, it's more
complicated than that.

00:24:26.240 --> 00:24:29.940
But the reason it is defined the
way it is

00:24:29.940 --> 00:24:33.840
will become clear over the next
few lectures because the very

00:24:33.840 --> 00:24:36.780
specific definition of matrix
multiplication unlocks lots of

00:24:36.780 --> 00:24:38.280
cool properties.

00:24:38.480 --> 00:24:41.240
So before we do the
multiplication,

00:24:41.240 --> 00:24:44.020
what I want to introduce is what
I sometimes call,

00:24:44.020 --> 00:24:46.240
and this is how I've written in
the notes,

00:24:46.240 --> 00:24:49.990
the golden rule for matrix
multiplication.

00:24:55.660 --> 00:24:56.660
OK.

00:24:57.740 --> 00:25:03.600
Here's what it says.
In order to multiply

00:25:03.600 --> 00:25:06.340
AB,

00:25:10.520 --> 00:25:11.800
here's the rule.

00:25:11.800 --> 00:25:17.540
The number of columns in A has
to equal the

00:25:17.540 --> 00:25:20.980
number of rows

00:25:22.320 --> 00:25:23.820
in B.

00:25:27.400 --> 00:25:28.040
Again,

00:25:28.040 --> 00:25:32.540
it's hard to predict that this
is what was coming.

00:25:35.020 --> 00:25:38.680
So let me move this down a
little.

00:25:38.680 --> 00:25:39.880
Why don't we say A

00:25:39.880 --> 00:25:50.260
has

00:25:50.260 --> 00:25:51.260
shape?

00:25:51.480 --> 00:25:56.020
n by d, which has been our
default shape.

00:25:56.020 --> 00:25:58.260
Based on this rule,

00:25:58.260 --> 00:26:03.510
what must the shape of B be?

00:26:03.600 --> 00:26:06.420
If I need the number of columns
in A to equal the number of rows

00:26:06.420 --> 00:26:07.480
in B,

00:26:07.480 --> 00:26:10.740
what does the shape of B need to
be?

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:13.000
D by something, right?

00:26:13.000 --> 00:26:16.540
It doesn't say that the number
of columns in B needs to be

00:26:16.540 --> 00:26:17.540
anything,

00:26:18.360 --> 00:26:22.060
It's the number of rows in B
needs to be equal to D.

00:26:22.060 --> 00:26:22.800
Okay,

00:26:22.800 --> 00:26:24.300
so D

00:26:25.520 --> 00:26:29.560
has to be the number of rows of
B and the number of columns of B

00:26:29.560 --> 00:26:31.740
can be some other thing.
Let's call it P.

00:26:31.740 --> 00:26:32.160
Okay,

00:26:32.160 --> 00:26:35.540
this is the key here

00:26:35.540 --> 00:26:36.540
These

00:26:37.480 --> 00:26:41.500
The number of columns in A,
which is D The first thing I've

00:26:41.500 --> 00:26:44.280
highlighted and the number of
rows in B, the second thing I've

00:26:44.280 --> 00:26:44.940
highlighted,

00:26:44.940 --> 00:26:47.360
those are sometimes called

00:26:47.360 --> 00:26:49.610
The inner dimensions.

00:26:55.980 --> 00:26:58.160
OK, why is it the inner
dimensions? Well, it's when you

00:26:58.160 --> 00:27:01.260
write n by d next to d by p,

00:27:01.260 --> 00:27:02.880
the two d's are next to each
other.

00:27:02.880 --> 00:27:06.220
They're the inside of the pair
of the dimensions.

00:27:06.220 --> 00:27:09.220
Whereas the outer dimensions
would be what?

00:27:11.980 --> 00:27:13.700
What would the outer dimensions
be?

00:27:13.700 --> 00:27:15.120
Outer dimensions?

00:27:15.120 --> 00:27:18.620
Yeah, in this specific case, if
the inner dimensions are D and

00:27:18.620 --> 00:27:21.060
D, what are the outer
dimensions?

00:27:21.060 --> 00:27:23.310
N and P.

00:27:23.520 --> 00:27:24.520
So,

00:27:25.880 --> 00:27:28.130
the product AB

00:27:29.720 --> 00:27:31.220
has G.

00:27:32.760 --> 00:27:34.760
N by P.

00:27:34.760 --> 00:27:36.380
So those outer dimensions
matter.

00:27:36.380 --> 00:27:39.640
It's because they tell you the
number of rows in the product

00:27:39.640 --> 00:27:42.080
and the number of columns in the
product.

00:27:42.080 --> 00:27:45.940
So you can think of the inner
dimensions as collapsing or

00:27:45.940 --> 00:27:48.940
canceling each other out.

00:27:50.920 --> 00:27:55.660
So far you have no reason to
know why this definition is

00:27:55.660 --> 00:27:58.140
needed or why this golden rule
