00:59:40.830 --> 00:59:42.650
AV plus BV.

00:59:42.650 --> 00:59:43.610
That's true.

00:59:43.610 --> 00:59:48.570
And it also means A times the
vector sum u plus v is equal to

00:59:48.570 --> 00:59:50.530
a u plus a v.

00:59:50.530 --> 00:59:53.610
Both of those are true because
they're just special cases of

00:59:53.610 --> 00:59:57.360
the statement I've just written.

00:59:58.750 --> 00:59:59.750
Okay.

01:00:00.590 --> 01:00:02.190
Here's the big one.

01:00:02.190 --> 01:00:05.190
It is not commutative.

01:00:07.230 --> 01:00:09.480
So in general,

01:00:10.530 --> 01:00:16.530
a b is not equal to b a.

01:00:18.050 --> 01:00:21.530
This is huge because it is one
of the first times you've

01:00:21.530 --> 01:00:25.150
encountered a form of
multiplication that isn't

01:00:25.150 --> 01:00:26.150
commutative.

01:00:27.470 --> 01:00:31.950
Someone actually submitted a
note to update something in the

01:00:31.950 --> 01:00:33.970
notes where I said this is the
first time you've seen

01:00:33.970 --> 01:00:37.910
multiplication like this but the
cross product is also not

01:00:37.910 --> 01:00:41.330
commutative because if you
reverse the and makes the other

01:00:41.330 --> 01:00:42.510
vector negative.

01:00:42.510 --> 01:00:46.350
So u cross v is the same as
negative v cross u.

01:00:46.350 --> 01:00:48.570
But as we're seeing with AB and
BA,

01:00:48.570 --> 01:00:50.820
they're totally different.

01:00:51.430 --> 01:00:52.610
And most often,

01:00:52.610 --> 01:00:54.930
if you can multiply AB,

01:00:54.930 --> 01:00:58.110
it isn't even guaranteed that
you can multiply BA.

01:00:58.110 --> 01:00:59.810
And that's a consequence of our
golden rule.

01:00:59.810 --> 01:01:05.630
Okay, so suppose A is N by D,

01:01:05.630 --> 01:01:10.130
and B is D by P,

01:01:10.230 --> 01:01:13.980
AB is a valid product,

01:01:16.110 --> 01:01:19.650
because those inner dimensions
match.

01:01:22.010 --> 01:01:25.010
But what about BA?

01:01:25.950 --> 01:01:28.610
Note that this is asking for a
fundamentally different thing.

01:01:28.610 --> 01:01:32.360
AB is asking for linear
combinations of A's columns.

01:01:33.490 --> 01:01:37.590
BA would be asking for linear
combinations of B's columns.

01:01:37.590 --> 01:01:39.530
There's no reason to think that
these would need to end up being

01:01:39.530 --> 01:01:41.030
the same.

01:01:41.250 --> 01:01:43.950
But even if we just look at the
dimensions,

01:01:43.950 --> 01:01:46.200
sorry, the shapes,

01:01:46.250 --> 01:01:49.250
B is D by P,

01:01:49.250 --> 01:01:53.990
and A is N by D.

01:01:58.050 --> 01:02:03.790
And no one required p, which is
the number of columns of b,

01:02:03.930 --> 01:02:06.010
and n, which is the number of
rows of a,

01:02:06.010 --> 01:02:08.790
to be the same.

01:02:08.790 --> 01:02:14.310
So this product, ba, is only
valid if n is equal to p.

01:02:16.730 --> 01:02:22.590
So the first thing to note is
that just because ab is

01:02:22.590 --> 01:02:23.590
valid,

01:02:27.050 --> 01:02:30.800
BA doesn't have to be.

01:02:32.850 --> 01:02:34.850
Now typically at this point I
ask,

01:02:34.850 --> 01:02:39.210
okay so what for what kind of
matrix do for what kind of

01:02:39.210 --> 01:02:42.670
matrices I should say are both
AB and BA valid?

01:02:42.670 --> 01:02:44.870
And the answer you're probably
thinking of is well if they're

01:02:44.870 --> 01:02:46.670
both square then you could do it
both ways.

01:02:46.670 --> 01:02:49.010
And And that's true, but that's
not the only case in which you

01:02:49.010 --> 01:02:52.760
could multiply them both ways.

01:02:53.210 --> 01:02:58.930
All we needed was that P, which
is the number of columns in B,

01:02:59.170 --> 01:03:01.530
is equal to n, which is the
number of rows in A.

01:03:01.530 --> 01:03:04.010
But it's not necessary that
those numbers are also the same

01:03:04.010 --> 01:03:05.510
as D.

01:03:05.510 --> 01:03:09.710
So for example, we could have A
be 3 by 2 and B be 2 by 3.

01:03:09.710 --> 01:03:11.510
And there you could multiply
them both ways.

01:03:11.510 --> 01:03:15.830
OK, so suppose A is 1, 0,

01:03:15.830 --> 01:03:16.150
2,

01:03:16.150 --> 01:03:16.550
1,

01:03:16.550 --> 01:03:18.410
3, 0, whatever.
Make up some numbers.

01:03:18.410 --> 01:03:21.310
This is a 3 by 2 matrix.

01:03:21.310 --> 01:03:24.570
Suppose B is a 2 by 3 matrix, 0,
0,

01:03:24.570 --> 01:03:25.470
1,

01:03:25.470 --> 01:03:27.720
5, 4, 7.

01:03:27.950 --> 01:03:30.730
That's a 2 by 3 matrix.

01:03:30.730 --> 01:03:35.230
What is the shape of AB?

01:03:38.290 --> 01:03:40.490
What is the shape of AB? 3.

01:03:40.490 --> 01:03:41.490
Yeah.

01:03:41.510 --> 01:03:42.690
3 by 3.

01:03:42.690 --> 01:03:44.550
3 by 3.

01:03:44.550 --> 01:03:48.630
And what's the shape of BA?

01:03:48.630 --> 01:03:51.810
You can see that by writing
them.

01:03:51.810 --> 01:03:52.670
Again,

01:03:52.670 --> 01:03:56.830
with this annotated notation,
you can put 2 by 3 next to B.

01:03:57.490 --> 01:04:00.350
Next to A, you can put 3 by 2.

01:04:00.350 --> 01:04:03.570
Because the inner dimensions are
the same, it's legal to

01:04:03.570 --> 01:04:04.430
multiply.

01:04:04.430 --> 01:04:07.050
But the dimensions or the shape
of the output will be the outer

01:04:07.050 --> 01:04:09.190
dimensions.
So B A will be what?

01:04:12.330 --> 01:04:15.270
What will the shape of B A be?

01:04:15.270 --> 01:04:15.870
Yeah.

01:04:15.870 --> 01:04:17.050
Two by two.

01:04:17.050 --> 01:04:18.050
Awesome.

01:04:18.470 --> 01:04:19.690
Which tells you,

01:04:19.690 --> 01:04:23.590
even if both of them exist, so
even if both B A and A B exist,

01:04:23.590 --> 01:04:25.790
they don't have to be the same
shape, which means they can't be

01:04:25.790 --> 01:04:27.170
equal.
You can't have a three by three

01:04:27.170 --> 01:04:30.210
matrix be the same thing as a
two by two matrix.

01:04:30.210 --> 01:04:33.870
So even if a, b,

01:04:33.870 --> 01:04:38.050
and b, a both exist,

01:04:39.910 --> 01:04:42.930
they are not necessarily

01:04:42.930 --> 01:04:48.490
the same shape.

01:04:50.030 --> 01:04:51.030
Okay.

01:04:52.190 --> 01:04:55.940
Now, what if they're both
square?

01:04:56.350 --> 01:04:58.600
Let's find out.
Let's just make up an example.

01:05:07.470 --> 01:05:13.190
So why don't we say A is one,
two, three, four,

01:05:13.190 --> 01:05:16.470
and B is five, six, seven,
eight.

01:05:16.470 --> 01:05:22.130
What is A times

01:05:22.130 --> 01:05:23.130
B?

01:05:26.330 --> 01:05:31.590
What's the first entry that is
the top left entry in A times B?

01:05:38.290 --> 01:05:39.890
Yeah, close,

01:05:44.390 --> 01:05:46.640
not exactly 15.

01:05:47.890 --> 01:05:49.390
Yeah, 19.
How'd you get 19?

01:05:51.350 --> 01:05:52.570
Good.
Remember we're taking dot

01:05:52.570 --> 01:05:56.470
products of A's rows with the
first column of B,

01:05:56.470 --> 01:05:58.390
right? So it'll be one times
two,

01:05:58.390 --> 01:05:59.090
sorry,

01:05:59.090 --> 01:06:02.270
one times five plus two times
seven.

01:06:02.270 --> 01:06:03.550
Do we agree?

01:06:03.550 --> 01:06:05.590
Okay, so we're gonna get a 19
there.

01:06:05.590 --> 01:06:07.150
What's the next entry?

01:06:07.150 --> 01:06:09.070
Well it's going to be still
we're working with the first

01:06:09.070 --> 01:06:11.730
column of B and we're gonna dot
it with the second row of A.

01:06:11.730 --> 01:06:16.980
So we're going to get 15 plus
28, which sounds like 43.

01:06:17.330 --> 01:06:20.230
So those came from here.

01:06:20.230 --> 01:06:24.250
And then the second two entries
are going to come from dotting

01:06:24.250 --> 01:06:27.390
the second column of B with the
rows of A.

01:06:27.390 --> 01:06:29.930
So what's the first entry going
to be? Looks like 6 plus 16,

01:06:29.930 --> 01:06:31.190
which is 22.

01:06:31.190 --> 01:06:36.440
Then the second will be 18 plus
32, which is 50.

01:06:37.850 --> 01:06:38.790
I'll admit,

01:06:38.790 --> 01:06:42.470
when you're dealing with two
matrices that are both n by n,

01:06:42.470 --> 01:06:44.990
it gets confusing what dot
products you're supposed to

01:06:44.990 --> 01:06:47.690
take, because it's actually
easier when they're not both

01:06:47.690 --> 01:06:50.190
square, because there's only one
way it could work.

01:06:50.190 --> 01:06:52.550
But here, you may accidentally
forget and take

01:06:52.550 --> 01:06:56.390
the dot products of A's columns
with B's rows or something,

01:06:56.390 --> 01:06:58.450
but that's not the same thing.

01:06:58.450 --> 01:07:00.950
So this is what AB would be.

01:07:00.950 --> 01:07:02.670
What about BA?

01:07:02.670 --> 01:07:04.350
We don't need to do the whole
calculation.

01:07:04.350 --> 01:07:07.210
Just one piece of it will tell
us they're not the same.

01:07:07.210 --> 01:07:12.460
What is the top left entry in
BA?

01:07:12.830 --> 01:07:16.580
How would we find it?

01:07:18.350 --> 01:07:19.450
23.
How'd you get that?

01:07:19.970 --> 01:07:23.250
It was like 1 times 5 plus 3
times 6.

01:07:23.250 --> 01:07:25.970
And why are you doing that?
Well, it's because now B is the

01:07:25.970 --> 01:07:28.110
left matrix and A is the right
matrix.

01:07:28.110 --> 01:07:31.430
And how that's supposed to work
is you take dot products of the

01:07:31.430 --> 01:07:34.610
rows of the first matrix with
the columns of the second.

01:07:34.610 --> 01:07:40.610
So now we're dotting 5 6 with 1
3 5 plus 18 is 23,

01:07:40.670 --> 01:07:45.270
which is not the same as 19

01:07:45.270 --> 01:07:48.670
Which tells you a B and BA even
when the two matrices are square

01:07:48.670 --> 01:07:52.090
don't need to be the same, okay

01:07:52.090 --> 01:07:55.090
so in general a

01:07:57.210 --> 01:08:00.770
B is not the same as BA

01:08:00.770 --> 01:08:04.370
And something I subtly mentioned
is that when you're multiplying

01:08:04.370 --> 01:08:05.870
two matrices,

01:08:12.050 --> 01:08:15.110
the first one is called the left
matrix and the second one is

01:08:15.110 --> 01:08:16.510
called the right matrix.

01:08:16.510 --> 01:08:18.730
So often we will say things
like,

01:08:18.730 --> 01:08:22.670
we will right multiply the
equation on both sides by

01:08:22.670 --> 01:08:23.310
something,

01:08:23.310 --> 01:08:26.350
or we will left multiply the
equation on both sides by

01:08:26.350 --> 01:08:27.690
something, because those are
different.

01:08:27.690 --> 01:08:30.710
You can't just say multiply the
order in which the operation

01:08:30.710 --> 01:08:32.150
happens matters

01:08:32.150 --> 01:08:34.690
And so say if we have an
equation and we need to perform

01:08:34.690 --> 01:08:38.430
an operation to both sides of it
We need to specify well

01:08:38.430 --> 01:08:41.650
How did we do that operation if
you multiply on the left on one

01:08:41.650 --> 01:08:43.590
side and on the right on the
other? Well, you didn't actually

01:08:43.590 --> 01:08:46.450
do the same thing to both sides

01:08:46.450 --> 01:08:47.450
Okay

01:08:47.550 --> 01:08:51.170
It's the same reason that you
know, if AV is a valid product

01:08:51.170 --> 01:08:56.420
Well, then VA is not right the
shapes here don't match up

01:08:56.790 --> 01:09:00.190
So this is left multiplying

01:09:00.190 --> 01:09:05.630
by A.

01:09:09.530 --> 01:09:10.530
Now,

01:09:12.390 --> 01:09:17.490
you might ask, is there a way to
multiply a matrix and a vector

01:09:17.490 --> 01:09:19.090
where the vector comes first?

01:09:19.090 --> 01:09:22.050
And the answer is that it
requires the vector to be

01:09:22.050 --> 01:09:24.350
written as a row vector.

01:09:24.350 --> 01:09:28.770
And we'll see an example of that
very briefly at the end of today

01:09:28.770 --> 01:09:31.010
before I get to

01:09:31.010 --> 01:09:36.260
Yeah, I'll try and get to this.
So this is not valid

01:09:37.810 --> 01:09:43.550
Okay, two quick examples I want
to introduce here the

01:09:43.550 --> 01:09:44.550
first

01:09:45.370 --> 01:09:49.870
Is this idea of an identity
matrix

01:09:50.170 --> 01:09:53.920
To illustrate what's going on
here

01:09:54.870 --> 01:09:58.070
Consider the matrix 1, 0, 0, 1.

01:09:58.070 --> 01:10:02.010
And suppose I multiply it by
some arbitrary vector in R2,

01:10:02.010 --> 01:10:03.010
AB.

01:10:04.730 --> 01:10:06.980
What comes out?

01:10:14.330 --> 01:10:18.080
What comes out of this
operation?

01:10:18.190 --> 01:10:19.370
AB? Yeah.

01:10:19.370 --> 01:10:23.120
It's the same vector AB.
Yeah, it's the same vector AB,

01:10:24.290 --> 01:10:25.290
OK?

01:10:25.730 --> 01:10:31.550
So multiplying AB by this matrix
1001 does nothing to

01:10:31.550 --> 01:10:32.250
AB.

01:10:32.250 --> 01:10:33.910
It comes out being the same.

01:10:33.910 --> 01:10:36.810
So you should think of this
matrix 1001

01:10:36.810 --> 01:10:40.390
as being the equivalent of
multiplying a number by 1.

01:10:40.390 --> 01:10:41.930
Doesn't change its value.

01:10:41.930 --> 01:10:46.110
And that neutral element in
whatever set of objects you're

01:10:46.110 --> 01:10:48.390
working with is called the
identity element.

01:10:48.390 --> 01:10:51.830
So one is called the
multiplicative identity when

01:10:51.830 --> 01:10:53.790
you're working with real
numbers.

01:10:53.790 --> 01:10:57.610
This matrix is called the
identity matrix, but there's not

01:10:57.610 --> 01:11:00.190
just one identity matrix.
This is the identity matrix that

01:11:00.190 --> 01:11:02.890
is two by two.

01:11:02.890 --> 01:11:05.650
But you can imagine there's also
a three by three identity

01:11:05.650 --> 01:11:07.110
matrix.
What does it look like?

01:11:12.330 --> 01:11:14.650
You have to guess what the three
by three identity matrix looks

01:11:14.650 --> 01:11:15.650
like.

01:11:22.450 --> 01:11:28.150
And the way you describe

01:11:28.150 --> 01:11:34.130
this is that the identity matrix
has ones on

01:11:34.130 --> 01:11:36.170
the diagonal.

01:11:40.700 --> 01:11:43.310
That's how you'd refer to the
placement of these ones here.

01:11:43.310 --> 01:11:48.970
And the diagonal is made up of
all entries whose row number is

01:11:48.970 --> 01:11:52.720
equal to their column number.

01:11:54.110 --> 01:11:55.390
So for example,

01:11:55.390 --> 01:11:57.510
this is row one,

01:11:57.510 --> 01:11:59.010
column one.

01:11:59.370 --> 01:12:01.750
This is row three,

01:12:01.750 --> 01:12:03.250
column three.

01:12:04.550 --> 01:12:07.830
And so the role of the identity
matrix is that it's that neutral

01:12:07.830 --> 01:12:10.830
element that when you multiply
it by anything, you get back the

01:12:10.830 --> 01:12:11.950
same result.

01:12:11.950 --> 01:12:13.470
That's true for a matrix,

01:12:13.470 --> 01:12:14.950
it's true for a vector.

01:12:14.950 --> 01:12:16.270
So for example,

01:12:16.270 --> 01:12:21.710
if I took the matrix 7, 4, 3, 2,

01:12:21.710 --> 01:12:24.010
multiplied it by 1, 0, 0, 1,

01:12:24.010 --> 01:12:26.690
whether I do that on the left or
on the right,

01:12:26.690 --> 01:12:28.070
that is,

01:12:28.070 --> 01:12:30.430
if the identity matrix appears
on the right, or if it appears

01:12:30.430 --> 01:12:31.590
on the left,

01:12:31.590 --> 01:12:36.840
I get back the same matrix I
started with.

01:12:39.230 --> 01:12:42.630
And so something we will see
late next week is, what is the

01:12:42.630 --> 01:12:44.390
inverse of a matrix?

01:12:44.390 --> 01:12:47.590
The inverse of a matrix is just
another matrix

01:12:47.590 --> 01:12:50.170
that when you multiply it by the
one you started with,

01:12:50.170 --> 01:12:53.190
you get the identity matrix.

01:12:53.190 --> 01:12:55.250
There's a bit more to it than
that,

01:12:55.250 --> 01:12:57.730
because only square matrices
have inverses.

01:12:57.730 --> 01:13:00.150
And that's partially because the
identity matrix is only a

01:13:00.150 --> 01:13:03.150
concept for square matrices.

01:13:03.770 --> 01:13:05.070
Why is that?

01:13:05.070 --> 01:13:09.570
Why is there no not square
identity matrix?

01:13:13.330 --> 01:13:14.310
Yeah.

01:13:14.310 --> 01:13:17.610
It seems like you wouldn't be
able to have a proper diagonal

01:13:17.610 --> 01:13:19.510
if it's not a square.

01:13:19.510 --> 01:13:20.510
Exactly.

01:13:20.610 --> 01:13:24.310
And it's also related to the
fact that the whole premise here

01:13:24.310 --> 01:13:28.550
is that after multiplying this
vector in R2 by my matrix,

01:13:28.870 --> 01:13:31.210
I also ended up with a vector in
R2.

01:13:31.210 --> 01:13:33.290
But if the matrix isn't square,

01:13:33.290 --> 01:13:36.730
then its shape changes after
getting multiplied by A.

01:13:36.730 --> 01:13:39.730
OK, so in general,

01:13:43.490 --> 01:13:46.830
if a is n by d,

01:13:46.830 --> 01:13:50.330
and x is in R,

01:13:50.330 --> 01:13:53.410
again, remind me what the shape
of x must be to multiply it by

01:13:53.410 --> 01:13:54.410
a.

01:13:55.370 --> 01:13:55.810
d,

01:13:55.810 --> 01:13:57.090
it must be an Rd.

01:13:57.090 --> 01:14:02.190
Then ax is in Rn.
And what

01:14:02.190 --> 01:14:07.470
that means is unless A is a
square matrix,

01:14:08.490 --> 01:14:10.370
then x's shape changes.

01:14:10.370 --> 01:14:14.870
x and Ax have different shapes.

01:14:29.530 --> 01:14:31.290
Do we agree?

01:14:31.290 --> 01:14:31.730
OK.

01:14:31.730 --> 01:14:34.730
What that means is if the whole
role of the identity matrix is

01:14:34.730 --> 01:14:37.810
to be something that when you
multiply it by other things,

01:14:37.810 --> 01:14:40.150
you get the same result back,

01:14:40.150 --> 01:14:41.890
well then it inherently needs to
be square.

01:14:41.890 --> 01:14:44.170
Otherwise, if I multiply it by a
vector, it would change its

01:14:44.170 --> 01:14:46.210
shape, which means it can't be
the same thing after being

01:14:46.210 --> 01:14:47.210
multiplied.

01:14:49.210 --> 01:14:52.610
This is something we will
emphasize more next Thursday and

01:14:52.610 --> 01:14:53.770
after the break,

01:14:53.770 --> 01:14:58.110
this idea of thinking of the act
of multiplying the matrix by a

01:14:58.110 --> 01:15:01.110
vector as transforming the
vector.

01:15:01.170 --> 01:15:03.330
The default interpretation
you've probably built up in your

01:15:03.330 --> 01:15:05.870
minds right now is what we are
doing is we're taking linear

01:15:05.870 --> 01:15:07.550
combinations of the matrix's
columns.

01:15:07.550 --> 01:15:09.370
And that's what we're going to
explore more on Tuesday.

01:15:09.370 --> 01:15:12.750
But the other way to think about
this is,

01:15:12.750 --> 01:15:15.650
you can think of x as being
passed into a function.

01:15:15.650 --> 01:15:18.170
Or the thing we are doing to x
is we're just multiplying it by

01:15:18.170 --> 01:15:18.890
a.

01:15:18.890 --> 01:15:20.210
And as we will see,

01:15:20.210 --> 01:15:21.030
that does,

01:15:21.030 --> 01:15:22.230
in some cases,

01:15:22.230 --> 01:15:25.070
some describable thing to x.

01:15:25.070 --> 01:15:29.290
Like the kinds of things we will
say is that it rotates x, or it

01:15:29.290 --> 01:15:32.830
stretches x, or it reflects it,
or it projects it.

01:15:32.830 --> 01:15:36.450
These are all types of things
that a could do to x, depending

01:15:36.450 --> 01:15:39.330
on what a is.
And we will study that more next

01:15:39.330 --> 01:15:40.330
week.

01:15:41.690 --> 01:15:47.270
The other thing I wanted to
briefly mention is the transpose

01:15:47.270 --> 01:15:48.850
operation.

01:15:53.010 --> 01:15:58.750
So suppose I have a 3 by 2
matrix A.

01:15:58.750 --> 01:16:01.070
These components are 7, 0, 2,

01:16:01.070 --> 01:16:03.010
1, 0, 1.

01:16:03.010 --> 01:16:05.250
They don't need to have all
these zeros or else just put

01:16:05.250 --> 01:16:08.190
something random here in pi.

01:16:08.190 --> 01:16:10.310
The transpose of A,

01:16:10.310 --> 01:16:15.510
think of it as like A's friend
who is defined by turning A's

01:16:15.510 --> 01:16:17.870
rows into columns and

01:16:17.870 --> 01:16:22.370
A's columns into rows and it
looks like this exponent T here.

01:16:22.910 --> 01:16:25.990
Though it's not an exponent.

01:16:25.990 --> 01:16:32.910
So if A is 3 by 2 What is A
transpose?

01:16:32.910 --> 01:16:34.710
2 by 3.

01:16:34.710 --> 01:16:37.690
Its first row is 702.

01:16:37.690 --> 01:16:40.410
Its second row is A's second
column,

01:16:40.410 --> 01:16:42.660
1 pi 1.

01:16:45.610 --> 01:16:47.450
Do we agree?

01:16:47.450 --> 01:16:47.990
Okay,

01:16:47.990 --> 01:16:50.730
now the kind of thing we're
gonna study quite a bit next

01:16:50.730 --> 01:16:54.230
week, and you're even gonna see
it in homework 5, is what does

01:16:54.230 --> 01:16:58.310
the matrix A transpose A
contain? This is a very common

01:16:58.310 --> 01:16:59.290
operation.
You're going to see it almost

01:16:59.290 --> 01:17:01.850
every class for the rest of the
semester.

01:17:01.850 --> 01:17:04.950
Something like A transpose A or
X transpose X.

01:17:04.950 --> 01:17:07.950
This does something describable
in English.

01:17:09.030 --> 01:17:12.250
Firstly, I guess here, what is
its shape going to be?

01:17:12.250 --> 01:17:16.330
What is A transpose A shape
going to be?

01:17:16.330 --> 01:17:17.330
Yeah.

01:17:18.330 --> 01:17:19.730
Not quite.

01:17:19.730 --> 01:17:21.830
It's a little tricky because A
transpose is being written

01:17:21.830 --> 01:17:23.990
first, but here I'm seeing A
first.

01:17:23.990 --> 01:17:28.390
Yeah, so indeed, it's going to
be 2 by 2.

01:17:28.390 --> 01:17:30.640
Now, in English,

01:17:30.650 --> 01:17:33.650
what does it contain?

01:17:37.810 --> 01:17:40.910
It contains two rows and two
columns, but I more mean,

01:17:40.910 --> 01:17:44.070
you could describe the entries
of A transpose A, of which there

01:17:44.070 --> 01:17:45.330
will be four,

01:17:45.330 --> 01:17:48.490
in English to someone who was in
this class but didn't come to

01:17:48.490 --> 01:17:49.990
lecture today.

01:17:51.090 --> 01:17:54.840
How could you describe them?

01:17:56.010 --> 01:17:57.010
Awesome.

01:17:57.870 --> 01:18:03.120
Is it going to be like the
elements square?

01:18:03.230 --> 01:18:06.230
I see.
I think you have generally the

01:18:06.230 --> 01:18:07.250
right idea.

01:18:07.250 --> 01:18:09.070
Let me move them around so it's
a little more clear because

01:18:09.070 --> 01:18:12.150
we're multiplying A transpose on
the left.

01:18:12.150 --> 01:18:14.470
Though AA transpose is also a
valid thing.

01:18:14.470 --> 01:18:15.690
What are the entries going to
be?

01:18:15.690 --> 01:18:17.730
A dot product itself.

01:18:17.730 --> 01:18:22.070
Yeah, it's the dot products of
all pairs of A's columns.

01:18:22.070 --> 01:18:24.210
OK, let's check it out.
What's the first entry going to

01:18:24.210 --> 01:18:25.210
be?

01:18:25.330 --> 01:18:28.050
Well, it's going to be the first
row of A transpose dotted with

01:18:28.050 --> 01:18:29.630
the first column of A.

01:18:29.630 --> 01:18:32.470
But that's 702 dotted with 702.

01:18:32.470 --> 01:18:34.370
What's the second thing going to
be?

01:18:34.370 --> 01:18:37.590
Well, it's going to be still the
first column of A dotted with

01:18:37.590 --> 01:18:40.050
the second row of A transpose.
Remember, the rows of A

01:18:40.050 --> 01:18:41.810
transpose are just the columns
of A.

01:18:41.810 --> 01:18:42.770
So down here,

01:18:42.770 --> 01:18:47.730
I'm going to get 1 pi 1 dotted
with 702.

01:18:47.730 --> 01:18:50.510
Then I'm going to take the
second column and dot it with

01:18:50.510 --> 01:18:52.250
the first and second once again.

01:18:52.250 --> 01:18:56.570
So what that tells you is these
entries are going to be the

01:18:56.570 --> 01:18:56.990
same,

01:18:56.990 --> 01:18:59.490
because they're both going to be
the dot product of A's first

01:18:59.490 --> 01:19:01.150
column with A's second column.

01:19:01.150 --> 01:19:06.910
They're both going to be 7 plus
2, which is

01:19:06.910 --> 01:19:07.910
9.

01:19:10.010 --> 01:19:13.870
Meanwhile, the top left entry is
going to be 49 plus 4, that's

01:19:13.870 --> 01:19:15.610
going to be 53.

01:19:15.610 --> 01:19:17.490
At the bottom right one, okay
this one's going to look stupid,

01:19:17.490 --> 01:19:22.650
but it's going to be like pi
squared plus 2.

01:19:22.650 --> 01:19:26.470
Are we clear on where these
numbers came from?

01:19:26.470 --> 01:19:32.050
A transpose A contains the dot
products of

01:19:32.050 --> 01:19:36.650
all pairs of A's columns.

01:19:39.430 --> 01:19:41.790
We're going to use that a lot
this semester, so this is

01:19:41.790 --> 01:19:43.930
something to internalize.
OK,

01:19:43.930 --> 01:19:46.850
now if A transpose A contains
the dot products of pairs of A's

01:19:46.850 --> 01:19:47.850
columns,

01:19:48.090 --> 01:19:52.430
how do we get the dot products
of pairs of A's rows?

01:19:52.430 --> 01:19:55.430
You had to guess.

01:19:56.250 --> 01:19:57.010
Yeah?

01:19:57.010 --> 01:19:59.530
Doing AA transpose.
Exactly, you do AA transpose.

01:20:01.390 --> 01:20:02.390
Now,

01:20:02.690 --> 01:20:05.970
an important fact to know about
the transpose operation is that

01:20:05.970 --> 01:20:08.230
the transpose of a product, and
you can read more about this in

01:20:08.230 --> 01:20:09.730
the notes,

01:20:10.170 --> 01:20:15.050
flips the order of the product.
So AB transpose is equal to B

01:20:15.050 --> 01:20:17.300
transpose A transpose.

01:20:17.950 --> 01:20:22.090
And the last thing I wanted to
say before we fully run out of

01:20:22.090 --> 01:20:27.150
time here is that you can write
the dot product of two vectors,

01:20:27.150 --> 01:20:30.730
which remember requires them to
be column vectors,

01:20:30.730 --> 01:20:33.510
in terms of this transpose.

01:20:33.510 --> 01:20:37.260
How might you do that?

01:20:43.130 --> 01:20:48.690
Let's just pretend I have the
vector 1, 2, 3 dotted with

01:20:48.690 --> 01:20:50.940
7, 4, 7.

01:20:52.090 --> 01:20:55.470
Can you think of how to write
this in terms of matrix

01:20:55.470 --> 01:20:56.470
multiplication?

01:20:59.770 --> 01:21:01.530
Without a dot product?

01:21:01.530 --> 01:21:04.450
Because it seems like the matrix
multiplication has a dot product

01:21:04.450 --> 01:21:05.950
built in.

01:21:06.310 --> 01:21:08.560
You transpose v?

01:21:09.270 --> 01:21:10.670
Close.
It's you translate, did you say

01:21:10.670 --> 01:21:12.270
U or V?

01:21:12.270 --> 01:21:12.690
Like,

01:21:12.690 --> 01:21:14.490
U transpose.
Good, good, good.

01:21:15.190 --> 01:21:19.690
What you can do is imagine
rotating the first vector

01:21:20.150 --> 01:21:22.400
using the transpose.

01:21:22.870 --> 01:21:24.430
And now you don't need a dot
product.

01:21:24.430 --> 01:21:26.350
Remember, the dot product only
works between two column

01:21:26.350 --> 01:21:27.790
vectors.
So it would be wrong to put a

01:21:27.790 --> 01:21:29.030
dot there,

01:21:29.030 --> 01:21:30.030
OK?

01:21:32.310 --> 01:21:33.810
No dot.

01:21:33.970 --> 01:21:36.230
But this is just regular matrix
multiplication.

01:21:36.230 --> 01:21:40.190
I have a 1 by 3 matrix and a 3
by 1 matrix.

01:21:40.190 --> 01:21:43.290
The result is 1 by 1 and it
comes from taking the dot

01:21:43.290 --> 01:21:46.390
product of the first thing's
only row with the second thing's

01:21:46.390 --> 01:21:49.330
only column, which is 7 plus 8
plus 21,

01:21:49.330 --> 01:21:51.580
which is 30.

01:21:54.110 --> 01:21:56.010
The number itself is not
important.

01:21:56.010 --> 01:22:00.810
What I wanted to get at is this
is the transpose of u multiplied

01:22:00.810 --> 01:22:02.310
by v.

01:22:02.590 --> 01:22:05.350
Okay? And what that means is
there's lots of ways to write

01:22:05.350 --> 01:22:06.410
the dot product.

01:22:06.410 --> 01:22:07.950
There's u dot v,

01:22:07.950 --> 01:22:09.890
there's u transpose v,

01:22:09.890 --> 01:22:11.530
there's v transpose u,

01:22:11.530 --> 01:22:15.280
and there's v dot u.
Those are all the same.

